Eight of the nine commits in this branch change something a user has to
know about, and none of it was written down anywhere they would look. Two
new sections, both above the Reference line where people actually read.

"What gets committed, and why it matters" states the rule the whole memory
design rests on and that nothing in the README said out loud: .ariadne/ is
gitignored and disposable, so ADRs, graph assertions and insights live in
your repos or they do not survive. A table of the three files, who writes
each, and the answer to "do I commit this" (yes, all three). Then the parts
that surprise people: the tools leave the worktree dirty on purpose, writes
anchor to the repo owning the evidence in a multi-repo workspace, an
ambiguous write is refused rather than dropped somewhere git cannot see, and
reviewing an insight means reading it as an instruction to a machine.

"Settings" collects every knob in one table with defaults, instead of one
prose sentence listing names and a separate file holding the meanings.
Adds the two settings this branch introduced (maxInsights,
maxInsightChars), the VS Code pair, and the only environment variables that
do anything — all of them enrichment's, which is the honest way to say that
nothing else phones home.

Also says plainly that tool descriptions are written for agents rather than
for humans, and points at TOOLS.md and the aegis-help skill, so a reader who
finds them terse knows that is deliberate and where the long form went.

The "Using it" table now ends by saying three of those answers write a file
you should commit, since that is the one habit the toolkit needs and the
table read as though everything happened invisibly.
